 ## Overview
-Statistical Depth Refinement is a post-processing step applied during the training of radiance fields to mitigate artifacts caused by corrupt stereo depth inputs, such as those arising from fuzzy pixels and specular highlights [S1]. It prevents the model from overfitting to these noisy depth values by replacing them with smoother depth estimates derived from the underfitting state of the network [S1]. Unlike direct depth supervision which propagates noise, this method leverages the statistical distribution of residuals to identify and patch corrupt pixels, distinguishing it from standard filtering techniques by using the model's own early-stage outputs as a prior [S1].
+Statistical Depth Refinement is a post-processing step applied during radiance field training to mitigate artifacts caused by corrupt stereo depth inputs, such as those arising from fuzzy pixels and specular highlights [S1]. It prevents the model from overfitting to these noisy depth values by replacing them with smoother depth estimates derived from the network's early-stage (underfitting) outputs [S1]. Unlike direct depth supervision which propagates noise, this method leverages the statistical distribution of residuals to identify and patch corrupt pixels, distinguishing it from standard filtering techniques by using the model's own learning dynamics as a prior [S1].
 
